        <div class="cav">Same money, different deal</div>
        <h2 id="contract">Is a contract rate really the same as a salary?</h2>
        <p>No, because a 1099 contract and a W-2 salary are taxed and supported very differently, even at the same headline number. An employee splits the payroll tax with their employer and pays 7.65% of wages. A self-employed contractor pays both halves as self-employment tax, a combined 15.3% on net earnings, since the IRS sets that rate as 12.4% for Social Security plus 2.9% for Medicare. A contractor also carries their own health insurance, retirement, and unpaid time off, none of which a salary figure reveals. So a $65 an hour contract that grosses about $135,200 over a full year is not equal to a $135,200 salary: after the extra tax and lost benefits it lands closer to a low-six-figure W-2 role. The Best Answer Hub Salary Converter puts the W-2 and 1099 views side by side so an offer can be compared on the same footing rather than on the sticker rate.</p>

        <div class="cav">The honest part</div>
        <h2 id="private">Why do salary sites ask for so much personal information?</h2>
        <p>Because for many of them your personal data is the product, which is why the Best Answer Hub Salary Converter asks for none of it. Pure conversion needs nothing about you, since you already know the number you are converting. The friction appears on salary-benchmarking sites, whose business is collecting your pay history. Salary.com sells a personalized Personal Salary Report for roughly $30 to $80 and asks for your job title, employer size, industry, direct reports, experience, and education first. Glassdoor's Know Your Worth tool asks you to enter your current job title, employer, base salary, and location. PayScale routes you through a detailed profile survey before it shows a tailored figure. Handing pay and employer details to a server creates a record that can be stored, shared, or breached, and the Federal Trade Commission's own guidance to businesses is blunt: if you do not have a legitimate need for sensitive personal information, do not collect it. Because the Best Answer Hub Salary Converter computes everything in your browser, there is no server-side copy of your pay to protect and nothing to sell.</p>
        <blockquote>A converter needs one number: the one you already have. <span class="accent">If a salary tool wants your employer and your current pay first, you are the product, not the user.</span></blockquote>

        <div class="cav">The honest comparison</div>
        <h2 id="different">How is it different from Salary.com, PayScale, or other converters?</h2>
        <p>The difference is that the Best Answer Hub Salary Converter does the full conversion, including overtime and W-2 versus 1099, asks nothing about you, and keeps every figure on your device, while the big salary sites gate a personalized result behind your data or a fee. To be fair, not every free tool harvests data: pure calculators like Omni Calculator and Calculator.net are also free and signup-free, and they are honest peers. The table sets the common experience next to this one.</p>
